1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,480
When a payment page says paid, has any money moved?

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:05,592
Picture a corner shop with a tab book.

3
00:00:05,642 --> 00:00:08,952
You take your bread, and the shopkeeper writes one line in the book.

4
00:00:09,002 --> 00:00:11,040
No cash changes hands yet.

5
00:00:11,090 --> 00:00:14,640
At the end of the week, you settle up for every line at once.

6
00:00:14,690 --> 00:00:16,704
A card payment works the same way.

7
00:00:16,754 --> 00:00:19,584
Two banks take part in every card payment.

8
00:00:19,634 --> 00:00:21,792
The shop's bank is the acquirer.

9
00:00:21,842 --> 00:00:27,072
It signs up shops, takes their payments and pays them out, and this course builds its system.

10
00:00:27,122 --> 00:00:29,496
The buyer's bank is the issuer.

11
00:00:29,546 --> 00:00:32,592
It gave the buyer the card, and it holds the buyer's money.

12
00:00:32,642 --> 00:00:37,416
Between them sits the card network, which carries messages from one bank to the other.

13
00:00:37,466 --> 00:00:45,048
When the buyer confirms the payment, the acquirer sends a question through the card network to the issuer: may this card pay this amount?

14
00:00:45,098 --> 00:00:48,216
The issuer checks the card and the money on the account.

15
00:00:48,266 --> 00:00:51,720
Then it answers yes or no, in a few seconds.

16
00:00:51,770 --> 00:00:54,144
That yes is a promise, written down.

17
00:00:54,194 --> 00:00:56,664
It is a line in the tab book, not money.

18
00:00:56,714 --> 00:00:58,536
The money moves later.

19
00:00:58,586 --> 00:01:04,656
Many payments travel together, in one batch, from the issuer through the card network to the acquirer.

20
00:01:04,706 --> 00:01:06,600
That batch is called settlement.

21
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:10,128
Then the acquirer keeps its fee and pays the shop the rest.

22
00:01:10,178 --> 00:01:14,976
So a payment has two moments: the yes, in seconds, and the money, later.

23
00:01:15,026 --> 00:01:18,264
Giving money back depends on which side of settlement you stand.

24
00:01:18,314 --> 00:01:21,336
Before settlement, no money has moved yet.

25
00:01:21,386 --> 00:01:25,512
So the line is simply crossed out, as if the payment never happened.

26
00:01:25,562 --> 00:01:27,384
That is a reversal.

27
00:01:27,434 --> 00:01:30,192
After settlement, the money has already moved.

28
00:01:30,242 --> 00:01:34,464
Crossing out is too late, so the money must be sent back as a new movement.

29
00:01:34,514 --> 00:01:36,336
That is a refund.

30
00:01:36,386 --> 00:01:38,208
Now watch the product.

31
00:01:38,258 --> 00:01:43,512
On the bank's payment page, the buyer types the card and confirms it with a code sent to their phone.

32
00:01:43,562 --> 00:01:48,000
The page says "Taking the payment", and below it, "Your bank is being asked".

33
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:51,888
A few seconds later it says "Paid", with a reference number.

34
00:01:51,938 --> 00:01:53,760
That is the issuer's yes.

35
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:57,168
Now open the merchant portal, where the shop's staff work.

36
00:01:57,218 --> 00:02:00,816
The Payments page lists every payment on the shop's websites.

37
00:02:00,866 --> 00:02:02,688
Open the paid one.

38
00:02:02,738 --> 00:02:07,296
It shows the amount, the bank's fee, and every call to the card network.

39
00:02:07,346 --> 00:02:11,328
It also shows "Refundable until" a date, and a "Give back" button.

40
00:02:11,378 --> 00:02:19,320
Press it, and a dialog explains the rule in one line: on the day of payment the money is reversed; later it is refunded.

41
00:02:19,370 --> 00:02:26,976
In the bank admin portal, on its own Payments page, a payment given back shows one of two different states: Reversed, or Refunded.

42
00:02:27,026 --> 00:02:28,848
They never share one word.

43
00:02:28,898 --> 00:02:38,160
And each morning, the merchant's Daily reports page adds up the day before: what was paid, the bank fee, what was refunded, and what the shop receives.

44
00:02:38,210 --> 00:02:40,032
What does this protect?

45
00:02:40,082 --> 00:02:43,320
Picture a system that treats the yes as money.

46
00:02:43,370 --> 00:02:49,392
It pays the shop at once, then the batch never brings that payment, and the bank has paid out money it never got.

47
00:02:49,442 --> 00:02:53,064
Now picture a system that mixes reversal and refund.

48
00:02:53,114 --> 00:02:58,080
It crosses out a line that was already settled, and the buyer's money never comes back.

49
00:02:58,130 --> 00:03:03,768
Or it sends a refund for a payment that was already crossed out, and the buyer is paid twice.

50
00:03:03,818 --> 00:03:08,640
Either way, someone loses money: the bank, the shop or the buyer.

51
00:03:08,690 --> 00:03:10,992
So two pairs always stay apart.

52
00:03:11,042 --> 00:03:12,864
Approved is not paid out.

53
00:03:12,914 --> 00:03:14,976
A reversal is not a refund.

54
00:03:15,026 --> 00:03:16,848
Back to the tab book.

55
00:03:16,898 --> 00:03:18,888
A line is written in seconds.

56
00:03:18,938 --> 00:03:20,880
The money moves at the end of the week.

57
00:03:20,930 --> 00:03:25,392
Before then, you cross a line out; after then, you hand the cash back.

58
00:03:25,442 --> 00:03:27,720
Next: what you may keep about a card.
